The phase inventory is now strongly supported: omitting WO₃ gives ~14.49% Rwp, omitting Mn₃WO₆ gives ~10.87%, while alternate WO₃ settings do not improve the three-phase fit. Adding Mn₃O₄ refines to only ~1.4 wt% and changes Rwp by ~0.015 percentage point, so it is not yet defensible as a real fourth phase. The dominant remaining residuals are intensity ratios within already assigned peaks, which points to atomic-coordinate/texture/profile refinement rather than missing peak positions.

Texture refinement resolves the largest intensity mismatch: MnWO₄ strongly selects March–Dollase [111] (ratio ≈1.16), and WO₃ strongly selects [010] (ratio ≈1.36), lowering Rwp from ~6.95% to ~4.39%. Mn₃WO₆ shows no axis-specific gain, so I’m keeping it random rather than fitting a weak, ambiguous texture term. I’m now re-optimizing calibration, cells, bounded size/strain, isotropic ADPs, and coordinates around that physically supported model.

The bounded refinement is now at ~3.33% Rwp with physically plausible three-phase fractions near 64% MnWO₄, 18% WO₃, and 18% Mn₃WO₆. Heavy-atom shifts remain small; oxygen-coordinate refinement helps modestly. I’m checking the generator-supported anisotropic-strain axes and a fourth-phase scale against this improved residual before freezing the final model.

The three-phase assignment is now stable: MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and Mn₃WO₆. I’m finishing the last profile-model comparison and then will export and independently replay-check the exact final GSAS-II project so the CIFs, fractions, and `fit.csv` all come from the same model.

